      "t": "Ce que j'ai dit était horrible.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "gabriel",
      "t": "Non, j'aurais dû te le dire.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "sofia",
      "t": "Tu veux boire quelque chose?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "gabriel",
      "t": "Oui, volontiers.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "sofia",
      "t": "J'ai du porto.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "gabriel",
      "t": "J'adore le porto.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "lea",
      "t": "Gabriel!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "gabriel",
      "t": "Andréa? Tu es blessée?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "lea",
      "t": "J'ai eu un problème de piscine!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "lea",
      "t": "Gabriel, épouse-moi!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "lea",
      "t": "Protège-moi, sauve-moi!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "lea",
      "t": "Aide-moi, Gabriel...",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Let's walk through Gabriel's apology and Andréa's midnight panic step by step.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "sofia",
      "t": "Oui?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes?' Sofia answers the door with a brief question.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "gabriel",
      "t": "Sofia? Excuse-moi, je te dérange?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Sofia? Sorry, am I disturbing you?' Gabriel uses the informal pronoun 'you' with the verb 'disturb'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "sofia",
      "t": "Non, tu ne me déranges pas.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'No, you aren't bothering me.' The standard negation 'not' wraps around the pronoun and verb.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "gabriel",
      "t": "Je devais te voir.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I had to see you.' Gabriel opens with the word for 'had to' and lands on 'to see' at the close.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "gabriel",
      "t": "Je me sens vraiment mal pour tout à l'heure.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I feel really bad about earlier.' The key word here means 'feel', paired with 'bad' to make the point clear.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "sofia",
      "t": "Ce que j'ai dit était horrible.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What I said was horrible.' The relative phrase 'that' means 'what' or 'that which'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "gabriel",
      "t": "Non, j'aurais dû te le dire.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'No, I should have told you.' Listen for the expression meaning 'no', which leads naturally into 'to tell'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "sofia",
      "t": "Tu veux boire quelque chose?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Do you want to drink something?' The conjugated verb 'want' is followed directly by an infinitive.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "gabriel",
      "t": "Oui, volontiers.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes, gladly.' The polite adverb 'gladly' accepts an invitation with pleasure.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "sofia",
      "t": "J'ai du porto.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I have some port.' The partitive article 'some' indicates an unspecified quantity of drink.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "gabriel",
      "t": "J'adore le porto.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I love port.' French verbs of preference take a definite article.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "lea",
      "t": "Gabriel!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Gabriel! Open up!' Andréa pounds frantically on Gabriel's apartment door late at night.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "gabriel",
      "t": "Andréa? Tu es blessée?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Andréa? Are you hurt?' The feminine participle 'hurt' agrees with Andréa.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "lea",
      "t": "J'ai eu un problème de piscine!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I had a pool problem!' Andréa explains her drenched clothes with frantic absurdity.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "lea",
      "t": "Gabriel, épouse-moi!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Gabriel, marry me!' Listen for the expression meaning 'marry me!', which leads naturally into 'marry me!'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "lea",
      "t": "Protège-moi, sauve-moi!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Protect me, save me!' Two urgent imperatives plead for emotional rescue.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "lea",
      "t": "Aide-moi, Gabriel...",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Help me, Gabriel...' Andréa whispers her final desperate plea in the hallway. Now listen to the whole scene again at normal speed, and see how much more you catch.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
